A(msq(5, 'racemisation', 'Which of these reactions give mainly a racemic mixture?', ['(R)-3-bromo-3-methylhexane boiled in water', '(S)-2-bromobutane with NaCN in DMSO', '(R)-1-bromo-1-phenylethane in aqueous ethanol', '(S)-2-bromooctane with concentrated NaOH in DMSO'], [0, 2],
  '<p>Reactions that go through a planar carbocation (' + SN1 + ') give racemisation: the tertiary bromide in water (A) and the benzylic bromide in aqueous ethanol (C). The other two are ' + SN2 + ' with a strong nucleophile in a polar aprotic solvent: they give clean inversion.</p>', 'A benzylic secondary halide in a polar protic solvent also goes by SN1.'));
A(msq(4, 'alkyl chlorides from alcohols', 'Which reagents convert CH3CH2OH into CH3CH2Cl?', ['SOCl2', 'PCl5', 'dry HCl with ZnCl2', 'NaCl with conc. H2SO4'].map(F), [0, 1, 2],
  '<p>Thionyl chloride (SO<sub>2</sub> and HCl are the by-products), PCl<sub>5</sub> (POCl<sub>3</sub> and HCl) and HCl with anhydrous ZnCl<sub>2</sub> (Groves process) all work. NaCl with sulphuric acid does not give good yields of chloroalkane: chloride is a poor nucleophile (and alkyl bromides are made with NaBr/H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> instead).</p>'));
A(msq(4, 'SN1 reactivity', 'Which of these halides do NOT undergo ' + SN1 + ' substitution readily?', ['CH2=CHCl', 'C6H5Cl', 'CH2=CHCH2Cl', 'C6H5CH2Cl'].map(F), [0, 1],
  '<p>The vinyl and aryl halides have an sp<sup>2</sup> carbon bonded to Cl, with a shorter, stronger C&ndash;Cl bond (partial double-bond character) and an unstable vinyl or aryl cation. Allyl and benzyl chlorides ionise easily because the carbocations are resonance stabilised.</p>', 'Allyl and vinyl look alike but behave in opposite ways.'));
A(msq(4, 'hydrolysis to acids', 'Which compounds give benzoic acid (after acidification) when boiled with aqueous NaOH?', ['C6H5CCl3', 'C6H5CH2Cl', 'C6H5CH2CH2Cl', 'C6H5COCl'].map(F), [0, 3],
  '<p>Benzotrichloride hydrolyses (through the unstable triol) to the carboxylate: benzoic acid. Benzoyl chloride is an acid chloride and gives benzoate. Benzyl chloride gives benzyl alcohol, and 2-phenylethyl chloride gives 2-phenylethanol (or styrene by elimination); neither gives an acid.</p>', 'Three chlorines on the side-chain carbon mean carboxylic acid; two mean aldehyde; one means alcohol.'));
A(msq(3, 'nitration of chlorobenzene', F('Which statements are correct for the nitration of chlorobenzene with a mixture of concentrated HNO3 and H2SO4?'), ['The major product is 1-chloro-4-nitrobenzene', 'The ring reacts more slowly than benzene', 'The major product is 1-chloro-3-nitrobenzene', 'Chlorine is deactivating (&minus;I) but ortho/para directing (+R)'], [0, 1, 3],
  '<p>The &minus;I effect of Cl withdraws electrons and slows the reaction. The lone pairs donate by resonance (+R) and stabilise attack at the ortho and para positions, so the para isomer (less crowded) is the major product. The meta product is minor.</p>'));
A(msq(4, 'carbylamine reaction', 'Which amines give a foul-smelling isocyanide when warmed with chloroform and alcoholic KOH?', ['CH3NH2', 'C6H5NH2', '(CH3)2NH', '(CH3)3N'].map(F), [0, 1],
  '<p>Only primary amines (RNH<sub>2</sub> and ArNH<sub>2</sub>) give the carbylamine reaction: CHCl<sub>3</sub> + RNH<sub>2</sub> + 3KOH &rarr; RNC + 3KCl + 3H<sub>2</sub>O, via dichlorocarbene. Secondary and tertiary amines do not.</p>', 'The test is for primary amines of both kinds, aliphatic and aromatic.'));
A(msq(5, 'preparation of iodides', 'Which routes give 1-iodopropane as the main product?', ['propan-1-ol with red P and I2', '1-chloropropane with NaI in acetone', 'propene with HI', 'propene with HBr and a peroxide, then NaI'].map(F), [0, 1, 3],
  '<p>Red P and iodine make PI<sub>3</sub> in situ, which converts the alcohol into the iodide (A). The Finkelstein reaction swaps Cl for I (B). Propene with HI follows Markovnikov and gives 2-iodopropane (C is wrong). With peroxide, HBr adds the anti-Markovnikov way to give 1-bromopropane, and NaI in acetone converts it to 1-iodopropane (D).</p>', 'HI never shows the peroxide effect; only HBr does.'));
A(msq(4, 'Grignard reagents and acidic hydrogens', 'Which of these destroy ethylmagnesium bromide by giving ethane?', ['water', 'ethanol', 'diethyl ether', 'phenylethyne (C6H5C&equiv;CH)'], [0, 1, 3],
  '<p>A Grignard reagent is a strong base and takes any acidic hydrogen: water, an alcohol and a terminal alkyne all protonate it to give ethane. Diethyl ether has no acidic hydrogen; it is the solvent that stabilises the reagent.</p>'));
A(msq(4, 'SN2 features', 'Which statements about an ' + SN2 + ' reaction are correct?', ['The rate is proportional to [RX][Nu]', 'The configuration at a chiral carbon is inverted', 'Tertiary halides react faster than primary ones', 'A polar protic solvent speeds it up for an anionic nucleophile'], [0, 1],
  '<p>' + SN2 + ' is bimolecular, with a single step and backside attack, so the rate depends on both reactants and the configuration is inverted (Walden inversion). Steric crowding makes tertiary halides the slowest. Protic solvents hydrogen-bond to the anion and lower its reactivity, so aprotic solvents are better.</p>'));
A(msq(4, 'geometrical isomerism', 'Which of the following can show geometrical (cis&ndash;trans) isomerism?', ['ClCH=CHCl', 'CH2=CCl2', '1,2-dichlorocyclobutane', 'CH3CH=CHCl'], [0, 2, 3],
  '<p>1,2-Dichloroethene and 1-chloroprop-1-ene each have two different groups on both doubly bonded carbons. 1,2-Dichlorocyclobutane has cis and trans forms (the ring stops rotation). 1,1-Dichloroethene has two identical Cl on one carbon, so it has no cis-trans isomers.</p>'));

// ---- integer (10) ----
A(nat(5, 'monochlorination of 2-methylbutane', 'Free-radical monochlorination of 2-methylbutane gives how many different products in all, counting enantiomers separately?', 6, 0, '', '<p>Constitutional isomers: 1-chloro-2-methylbutane (chiral), 2-chloro-2-methylbutane, 2-chloro-3-methylbutane (chiral) and 1-chloro-3-methylbutane.</p><p>Two of them are chiral and each gives 2 enantiomers: 2 + 1 + 2 + 1 = <b>6</b>.</p>', 'Remember to count both enantiomers of each chiral product.', () => 2 + 1 + 2 + 1));
A(nat(4, 'isomers of C4H9Br', 'Counting stereoisomers separately, the total number of isomeric bromoalkanes of formula C4H9Br is', 5, 0, '', '<p>Constitutional isomers: 1-bromobutane, 2-bromobutane, 1-bromo-2-methylpropane and 2-bromo-2-methylpropane = 4.</p><p>Only 2-bromobutane is chiral (an extra enantiomer): 4 + 1 = <b>5</b>.</p>', 'Four structural isomers but five in all.', () => 4 + 1));
A(nat(4, 'isomers of trichlorobenzene', 'The number of isomers of C6H3Cl3 (trichlorobenzene) is', 3, 0, '', '<p>The three positions on the ring: 1,2,3-trichloro, 1,2,4-trichloro and 1,3,5-trichloro. Others (such as 1,3,4) are the same compounds numbered differently.</p><p>Answer <b>3</b>.</p>', 'Check by renumbering; 1,2,6 is the same as 1,2,3.', () => 3));
A(nat(3, 'DDT', 'How many carbon atoms in one molecule of DDT, (ClC6H4)2CH&ndash;CCl3, are sp<sup>2</sup> hybridised?', 12, 0, '', '<p>The two benzene rings have 6 + 6 = 12 sp<sup>2</sup> carbons. The CH carbon and the CCl<sub>3</sub> carbon each have four single bonds: sp<sup>3</sup>.</p><p>Answer <b>12</b>.</p>', '', () => 6 + 6));
A(nat(5, 'isomers of C3H6Br2', 'The total number of isomeric dibromopropanes, C3H6Br2, including stereoisomers, is', 5, 0, '', '<p>Constitutional isomers: 1,1-, 1,2-, 1,3- and 2,2-dibromopropane = 4. Of these only 1,2-dibromopropane has a chiral carbon (C2: H, CH<sub>3</sub>, Br, CH<sub>2</sub>Br).</p><p>So 4 + 1 = <b>5</b>.</p>', 'Do not forget the enantiomer of 1,2-dibromopropane.', () => 4 + 1));
A(nat(3, 'hybridisation of C&ndash;X carbon', 'In how many of these is the carbon bonded to Cl sp<sup>2</sup> hybridised: CH2=CHCl, C6H5Cl, C6H5CH2Cl, CH2=CHCH2Cl, CH3CH=CHCl, ClCH2C&equiv;CH?', 3, 0, '', '<p>sp<sup>2</sup> C&ndash;Cl carbon: vinyl chloride (yes), chlorobenzene (yes), CH<sub>3</sub>CH=CHCl (yes). Benzyl chloride, allyl chloride and propargyl chloride have a CH<sub>2</sub>Cl carbon (sp<sup>3</sup>).</p><p>Answer <b>3</b>.</p>', 'Allyl and benzyl halides are allylic or benzylic, not vinylic or aryl.', () => 3));
A(nat(5, 'counting alkenes', 'The number of different alkenes (counting E and Z isomers separately) that can be formed by dehydrobromination of 3-bromo-3-methylhexane, CH3CH2C(Br)(CH3)CH2CH2CH3, is', 5, 0, '', '<p>&beta;-Hydrogens lie on three different carbons.</p><p>(i) The CH<sub>3</sub> on C3: 2-ethylpent-1-ene (1). (ii) The CH<sub>2</sub> of the ethyl group: 3-methylhex-2-ene, E and Z (2). (iii) The CH<sub>2</sub> of the propyl group: 3-methylhex-3-ene, E and Z (2).</p><p>Total 1 + 2 + 2 = <b>5</b>.</p>', 'The two E/Z pairs make the count larger than the number of kinds of &beta;-carbon.', () => 1 + 2 + 2));
A(nat(3, 'monochlorination of butane', 'Monochlorination of butane (CH3CH2CH2CH3) gives how many different products, counting the enantiomers as separate?', 3, 0, '', '<p>1-Chlorobutane (1) and 2-chlorobutane, which is chiral, giving two enantiomers (2).</p><p>1 + 2 = <b>3</b>.</p>', '', () => 1 + 2));
A(nat(4, 'lone pairs', 'The total number of lone pairs of electrons on all the atoms of one molecule of CF2Cl2 is', 12, 0, '', '<p>Each F has 3 lone pairs and each Cl has 3. Carbon has none (four bond pairs).</p><p>2 &times; 3 + 2 &times; 3 = <b>12</b>.</p>', 'Each halogen in a single bond keeps three lone pairs.', () => 2 * 3 + 2 * 3));
A(nat(5, 'Wurtz stereoisomers', 'Equimolar 2-bromobutane and bromoethane are treated with sodium in dry ether. Counting all stereoisomers, the total number of different hydrocarbons formed is', 5, 0, '', '<p>Three couplings are possible. (i) sec-Butyl + sec-butyl: 3,4-dimethylhexane, with two stereocentres and two identical halves: (R,R), (S,S) and meso = 3 stereoisomers. (ii) sec-Butyl + ethyl: 3-methylpentane (achiral) = 1. (iii) Ethyl + ethyl: butane = 1.</p><p>3 + 1 + 1 = <b>5</b>.</p>', 'A coupling of two chiral groups gives diastereomers.', () => 3 + 1 + 1));

// ---- numeric (8) ----
A(nat(4, 'first-order hydrolysis of a tertiary halide', 'A tertiary bromide hydrolyses by ' + SN1 + ' with first-order rate constant 2.3 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup> s<sup>-1</sup>. Time for 75 % of it to react, in seconds, is', 602, 3, 's', '<p>75 % reacted means 25 % left: two half-lives. t<sub>1/2</sub> = 0.693/k = 0.693/(2.3 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup>) = 301.3 s.</p><p>Time = 2 &times; 301.3 = <b>602.6 s</b> (about 602).</p>', 'Two half-lives for 75 %, not one.', () => 2 * Math.LN2 / 2.3e-3));
A(nat(3, 'Sandmeyer yield', 'Aniline (C6H5NH2, 93 g mol<sup>-1</sup>), 9.3 g, is converted by diazotisation and the Sandmeyer reaction into chlorobenzene (112.5 g mol<sup>-1</sup>) in 70 % overall yield. Mass of chlorobenzene in grams is', 7.88, 0.05, 'g', '<p>Moles of aniline = 9.3/93 = 0.100. At 100 % yield, 0.100 mol of C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>Cl = 11.25 g.</p><p>At 70 %: 11.25 &times; 0.70 = <b>7.875 g</b>.</p>', 'The yield is of the final product, so apply it once.', () => 9.3 / 93 * 112.5 * 0.7));
A(nat(3, 'phosgene from chloroform', 'Chloroform, 23.9 g (119.5 g mol<sup>-1</sup>), is completely oxidised by air in light: 2CHCl<sub>3</sub> + O<sub>2</sub> &rarr; 2COCl<sub>2</sub> + 2HCl. Mass of phosgene (99 g mol<sup>-1</sup>) in grams is', 19.8, 0.1, 'g', '<p>Moles of CHCl<sub>3</sub> = 23.9/119.5 = 0.200. The ratio is 2 : 2, so 0.200 mol of COCl<sub>2</sub>.</p><p>Mass = 0.200 &times; 99 = <b>19.8 g</b>.</p>', '', () => 23.9 / 119.5 * 99));
A(nat(5, 'DDT synthesis', 'DDT (354.5 g mol<sup>-1</sup>) is made from chloral and chlorobenzene: 2C6H5Cl + CCl3CHO &rarr; DDT + H2O. From 22.5 g of chlorobenzene (112.5 g mol<sup>-1</sup>) with excess chloral and 100 % yield, the mass of DDT in grams is', 35.45, 0.2, 'g', '<p>Moles of chlorobenzene = 22.5/112.5 = 0.200. Two C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>Cl give one DDT, so 0.100 mol of DDT.</p><p>Mass = 0.100 &times; 354.5 = <b>35.45 g</b>.</p>', 'Two chlorobenzenes per DDT.', () => 22.5 / 112.5 / 2 * 354.5));
A(nat(3, 'chlorine in DDT', 'The percentage by mass of chlorine in DDT, C<sub>14</sub>H<sub>9</sub>Cl<sub>5</sub> (C = 12, H = 1, Cl = 35.5), is', 50.1, 0.2, '%', '<p>Molar mass = 14 &times; 12 + 9 + 5 &times; 35.5 = 168 + 9 + 177.5 = 354.5.</p><p>Chlorine = 177.5/354.5 &times; 100 = <b>50.1 %</b>.</p>', 'Five Cl atoms, not one.', () => 177.5 / 354.5 * 100));
A(nat(5, 'optical rotation of a partly racemised product', 'The pure inverted alcohol from an ' + SN1 + ' reaction has specific rotation +38&deg;. The product from an optically pure halide is 65 % inverted and 35 % retained alcohol. What is the specific rotation of the product mixture, in degrees?', 11.4, 0.1, '&deg;', '<p>Optical purity = excess of the major enantiomer = 65 &minus; 35 = 30 %.</p><p>Specific rotation = 0.30 &times; 38 = <b>11.4&deg;</b>.</p>', 'The retained 35 % cancels an equal amount of the inverted form; it is not simply 0.65 &times; 38.', () => (65 - 35) / 100 * 38));
A(nat(4, 'dipole moment of o-dichlorobenzene', 'Each C&ndash;Cl bond in chlorobenzene has a moment of 1.5 D along its bond axis. In a regular hexagonal ring the two C&ndash;Cl axes of o-dichlorobenzene make 60&deg; with each other. Its dipole moment in debye is', 2.6, 0.03, 'D', '<p>R = &radic;(&mu;<sup>2</sup> + &mu;<sup>2</sup> + 2&mu;<sup>2</sup>cos 60&deg;) = &mu;&radic;3 = 1.5 &times; 1.732 = <b>2.60 D</b>.</p>', 'For the para isomer the angle is 180&deg; and the moment is zero.', () => Math.sqrt(2 * 1.5 ** 2 + 2 * 1.5 ** 2 * Math.cos(Math.PI / 3))));
A(nat(4, 'selectivity of chlorination', 'In free-radical chlorination of propane at 300 &deg;C the relative reactivity of a secondary hydrogen to a primary hydrogen is 3.8 : 1. The percentage of 1-chloropropane in the monochloro products is', 44.1, 0.3, '%', '<p>Propane has 6 primary H and 2 secondary H. Relative amounts: 1-chloropropane 6 &times; 1 = 6; 2-chloropropane 2 &times; 3.8 = 7.6.</p><p>1-chloropropane = 6/(6 + 7.6) &times; 100 = <b>44.1 %</b>.</p>', 'Weigh by the number of hydrogens as well as their reactivity.', () => 6 / (6 + 2 * 3.8) * 100));

// ---- passages (2 x 3) ----
GRP.hx9 = { title: 'An aromatic chloride X', passage: F('<p>Compound X, C7H7Cl, gives a white precipitate at once with alcoholic silver nitrate at room temperature. Boiling X with aqueous NaOH gives an alcohol Y. Strong oxidation of Y gives benzoic acid.</p>') };
A(P(3, 'identifying a benzylic chloride', 'hx9', 'The alcohol Y is', 'phenylmethanol', ['2-methylphenol', '4-methylphenol', '1-phenylethanol'], '<p>X is benzyl chloride, C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>Cl. Hydrolysis gives C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH (phenylmethanol, benzyl alcohol), which oxidises to benzoic acid. Chlorotoluenes would not react so fast with AgNO<sub>3</sub>, and cresols would not oxidise to benzoic acid.</p>'));
A(P(4, 'identifying a benzylic chloride', 'hx9', 'X reacts immediately with silver nitrate because', 'the benzyl cation formed is stabilised by resonance with the ring', ['the C&ndash;Cl bond in X is stronger than in chlorobenzene', 'Cl is attached directly to an sp<sup>2</sup> ring carbon', 'the nitrate ion is a stronger nucleophile than hydroxide'], '<p>The CH<sub>2</sub>Cl carbon is sp<sup>3</sup>. Loss of Cl<sup>-</sup> gives the C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>CH<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup> cation, whose charge is spread over the ring (resonance). So ionisation, and then precipitation of AgCl, is fast.</p>', 'In chlorobenzene the Cl is on the ring carbon itself, which is why it does not react.'));
A(PN(4, 'isomers of C7H7Cl', 'hx9', 'The number of isomers of C7H7Cl that contain a benzene ring is', 4, 0, '', '<p>Benzyl chloride (side-chain Cl) and the three ring-substituted chlorotoluenes: ortho, meta and para.</p><p>1 + 3 = <b>4</b>.</p>', '', () => 1 + 3));
GRP.hx10 = { title: 'A chiral bromide A', passage: F('<p>Compound A, C5H11Br, is 2-bromopentane. Pure (R)-A is warmed with alcoholic KOH, giving alkene B in the largest amount. In a separate reaction (R)-A is treated with sodium cyanide in DMSO.</p>') };
A(PN(4, 'alkenes from A', 'hx10', 'How many different alkenes (counting geometrical isomers) can A give by dehydrobromination?', 3, 0, '', '<p>&beta;-Carbons: C1 (a methyl) gives pent-1-ene; C3 gives pent-2-ene, which exists as E and Z.</p><p>Total 1 + 2 = <b>3</b>.</p>', '', () => 1 + 2));
A(P(4, 'Zaitsev product', 'hx10', 'The major alkene B is', '(E)-pent-2-ene', ['pent-1-ene', '(Z)-pent-2-ene', '2-methylbut-2-ene'], '<p>Alcoholic KOH removes a &beta;-hydrogen so that the more substituted (Zaitsev) alkene forms: pent-2-ene (disubstituted) rather than pent-1-ene. The trans (E) isomer is more stable (less steric strain) and so is formed in the larger amount.</p>', '2-methylbut-2-ene has a different carbon skeleton.'));
A(P(5, 'stereochemistry of SN2', 'hx10', 'The product of the reaction of (R)-A with NaCN in DMSO is', '(S)-2-methylpentanenitrile', ['(R)-2-methylpentanenitrile', 'a racemic mixture of 2-methylpentanenitrile', 'pent-2-ene'], '<p>Cyanide in DMSO is a good nucleophile in a polar aprotic solvent and the substrate is secondary, so the reaction is ' + SN2 + ' with inversion. The priority order stays the same (CN has the highest priority just as Br did: CN &gt; propyl &gt; CH<sub>3</sub> &gt; H), so inversion of geometry changes the label from R to S.</p>', 'The descriptor changes here only because the priorities in product and reactant line up.'));

// ---- assertion (5) ----
A(asr(4, 'Swarts reaction', 'Alkyl fluorides are best made by the Swarts reaction (alkyl chloride or bromide heated with AgF), not by the Finkelstein reaction.', 'The Finkelstein reaction works because NaCl or NaBr is insoluble in dry acetone, which pulls the equilibrium to the iodide.', 1,
  '<p>A is true. R is also true and is the reason the Finkelstein reaction makes <i>iodides</i>, but it does not say why fluorides need a different method (NaF does not dissolve in acetone, and the C&ndash;F bond is strong). So R is true but is not the explanation of A.</p>'));
A(asr(3, 'elimination versus substitution', 'Ethyl bromide with aqueous KOH gives mainly ethene.', 'Alcoholic KOH contains the stronger base, C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>O<sup>-</sup>, which favours elimination.', 3,
  '<p>Aqueous KOH gives mainly substitution (ethanol), so A is false. In alcohol, KOH partly forms ethoxide, a stronger base, and elimination dominates: R is true.</p>'));
A(asr(5, 'SNi', '(S)-Butan-2-ol treated with SOCl<sub>2</sub> in the absence of pyridine gives (S)-2-chlorobutane (retention).', 'The chlorosulphite intermediate collapses to an ion pair, and chloride is delivered from the same face that the leaving group left.', 0,
  '<p>Without a base the chlorosulphite ester R&ndash;O&ndash;S(=O)Cl loses SO<sub>2</sub> in an ion pair and the chloride returns to the same face (SNi): retention. A and R are true and R explains A. With pyridine the chloride attacks from the back instead: inversion.</p>'));
A(asr(4, 'melting points of isomers', 'p-Dichlorobenzene has a higher melting point than o- and m-dichlorobenzene.', 'p-Dichlorobenzene has the highest dipole moment of the three isomers.', 2,
  '<p>A is true: the symmetric para isomer packs better in the crystal lattice (m.p. 54 &deg;C against &minus;17 &deg;C and &minus;25 &deg;C). R is false: the para isomer has zero dipole moment because the bond moments cancel.</p>'));
A(asr(2, 'chloroform', 'Chloroform is a good solvent for fats and oils.', 'Chloroform was widely used as an anaesthetic.', 1,
  '<p>Both statements are true. Chloroform is a non-polar-ish solvent that dissolves fats. Its anaesthetic use is a separate fact and does not explain the solvent property.</p>'));

// ---- statements (6) ----
A(stm(3, 'ambident ions', 'Bromoethane with KCN gives mainly propanenitrile.', 'Bromoethane with AgCN gives mainly the isocyanide, because AgCN is largely covalent and the nitrogen lone pair attacks.', 0,
  '<p>KCN is ionic, so the carbon end attacks and the nitrile forms. AgCN is covalent, so only the nitrogen lone pair is free to attack and the isocyanide forms. Both statements are correct.</p>'));
A(stm(4, 'solvent effect', 'SN2 reactions of anionic nucleophiles are faster in DMSO than in methanol.', 'In DMSO the nucleophile is strongly solvated by hydrogen bonding.', 1,
  '<p>DMSO is polar aprotic: it solvates cations but not anions, leaving the nucleophile &ldquo;bare&rdquo; and reactive. So I is correct. II is wrong: strong hydrogen-bond solvation of the anion happens in <i>methanol</i> (protic), not DMSO.</p>'));
A(stm(4, 'neopentyl halides', '1-Chlorobutane undergoes ' + SN2 + ' much faster than 1-chloro-2,2-dimethylpropane.', 'The slow rate of the neopentyl halide is due to the electron-donating effect of the three methyl groups on the &beta;-carbon.', 1,
  '<p>I is correct. II is wrong: the slowness is steric. The three methyl groups on the &beta;-carbon block backside attack. Their inductive effect is minor.</p>', 'Steric hindrance, not electronics, is the cause.'));
A(stm(3, 'bromination of chlorobenzene', 'Bromination of chlorobenzene with Br<sub>2</sub> and FeBr<sub>3</sub> gives mainly 1-bromo-3-chlorobenzene.', 'The chlorine atom directs incoming electrophiles to the ortho and para positions.', 2,
  '<p>Chlorine is an ortho/para director (+R from its lone pairs), so the main product is 1-bromo-4-chlorobenzene (with some ortho). Statement I is wrong and II is correct.</p>'));
A(cnt(4, 'counting correct statements', 'How many of these are correct? (i) AgNO<sub>2</sub> with bromoethane gives mainly nitroethane. (ii) Bromoethane with sodium in dry ether gives butane. (iii) Allyl bromide is more reactive than propyl bromide in SN1. (iv) Chlorobenzene with aqueous NaOH at room temperature gives phenol. (v) Carbon tetrachloride is the preferred fire extinguisher today.', ['2', '3', '4', '5'], 1,
  '<p>(i) True. (ii) True (Wurtz). (iii) True: the allyl cation is resonance stabilised. (iv) False: chlorobenzene needs about 623 K and 300 atm. (v) False: CCl<sub>4</sub> is toxic and ozone-depleting, so it is no longer used.</p><p>Three are correct.</p>'));
A(cnt(4, 'counting correct statements', 'How many are correct? (i) An SN2 reaction at a chiral carbon inverts its configuration (Walden inversion). (ii) A racemic mixture is optically inactive because of external compensation. (iii) An SN1 reaction at a chiral carbon always gives exactly 50:50 retention and inversion. (iv) A meso compound has stereocentres but is optically inactive. (v) E2 elimination needs an anti-periplanar H&ndash;C&ndash;C&ndash;X arrangement.', ['3', '4', '5', '2'], 1,
  '<p>(i) True. (ii) True: the two enantiomers cancel (external compensation); a meso compound cancels internally. (iii) False: ion-pair effects often give slightly more inversion. (iv) True. (v) True.</p><p>Four are correct: i, ii, iv and v.</p>', 'The word &ldquo;always&rdquo; in (iii) makes it false.'));

// ---- single answer (10) ----
A(mk(2, 'preparation', 'Which reagent converts ethanol to chloroethane and leaves only gases as by-products?', 'SOCl<sub>2</sub>', ['PCl<sub>5</sub>', 'dry HCl with ZnCl<sub>2</sub>', 'NaCl and conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>'], '<p>C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH + SOCl<sub>2</sub> &rarr; C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>Cl + SO<sub>2</sub> + HCl. Both by-products escape as gases, so the product is easy to purify. PCl<sub>5</sub> leaves liquid POCl<sub>3</sub>.</p>'));
A(mk(3, 'peroxide effect', 'Propene reacts with HBr in the presence of benzoyl peroxide to give mainly', '1-bromopropane', ['2-bromopropane', '1,2-dibromopropane', 'propan-1-ol'], '<p>The peroxide starts a free-radical chain. Br&bull; adds to the terminal carbon to give the more stable secondary radical, so H ends up on C2: the anti-Markovnikov product, CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>Br.</p>', 'Only HBr shows this effect.'));
A(mk(3, 'Hunsdiecker reaction', 'The silver salt of propanoic acid is heated with bromine in CCl<sub>4</sub>. The product is', 'bromoethane', ['1-bromopropane', '2-bromopropane', 'propanoyl bromide'], '<p>Hunsdiecker reaction: RCOOAg + Br<sub>2</sub> &rarr; RBr + CO<sub>2</sub> + AgBr. The product has one carbon fewer than the acid, so C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>COOAg gives C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>Br.</p>', 'The haloalkane is one carbon shorter than the acid.'));
A(mk(3, 'allylic bromination', 'Propene is warmed with N-bromosuccinimide (NBS) and a peroxide in CCl<sub>4</sub>. The main product is', '3-bromoprop-1-ene', ['1,2-dibromopropane', '2-bromopropane', '1-bromopropane'], '<p>NBS provides a very low, steady concentration of Br<sub>2</sub>, so a radical substitution at the allylic CH<sub>3</sub> beats addition across the C=C. The allyl radical gives CH<sub>2</sub>=CHCH<sub>2</sub>Br.</p>', 'The double bond is untouched.'));
A(mk(5, 'rearrangement', 'The addition of HBr to 3,3-dimethylbut-1-ene gives mainly', '2-bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane', ['2-bromo-3,3-dimethylbutane', '1-bromo-3,3-dimethylbutane', '1-bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane'], '<p>H<sup>+</sup> adds to C1, giving a secondary cation next to the t-butyl group. A methyl shift gives the more stable tertiary cation, (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>C<sup>+</sup>CH(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>, which takes Br<sup>-</sup>: 2-bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane.</p>', 'The unrearranged product is only a minor product.'));
A(mk(5, 'SN2 versus SNi', '(S)-Butan-2-ol reacts with SOCl<sub>2</sub> in the presence of pyridine. The main product is', '(R)-2-chlorobutane', ['(S)-2-chlorobutane', 'racemic 2-chlorobutane', 'but-2-ene only'], '<p>Pyridine converts the HCl formed into pyridinium chloride and frees Cl<sup>-</sup> to attack from the back in an ' + SN2 + ' step, giving inversion. The priorities are Cl (or OH) &gt; C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub> &gt; CH<sub>3</sub> &gt; H, so inversion of geometry changes S to R.</p>', 'Without pyridine the answer is the opposite (retention, S).'));
A(mk(2, 'leaving groups', 'Methyl iodide reacts with hydroxide faster than methyl chloride because', 'iodide is a better leaving group', ['the C&ndash;I bond is shorter', 'iodine is more electronegative than chlorine', 'methyl iodide is a tertiary halide'], '<p>The C&ndash;I bond is longer and weaker than C&ndash;Cl, and I<sup>-</sup> is a weak base, so it leaves easily. Iodine is the <i>least</i> electronegative of the two, and CH<sub>3</sub>I is not tertiary.</p>'));
A(mk(2, 'silver nitrate test', 'Which of these gives a white precipitate fastest with alcoholic AgNO<sub>3</sub> at room temperature?', 'C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>Cl', ['C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>Cl', 'CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>Cl', 'CH<sub>2</sub>=CHCl'], '<p>Benzyl chloride ionises easily to a stabilised cation. Chlorobenzene and vinyl chloride do not react, and propyl chloride only reacts slowly on warming.</p>'));
A(mk(4, 'Freund reaction', '1,3-Dibromopropane is heated with zinc dust. The product is', 'cyclopropane', ['propene', 'propane', 'propyne'], '<p>Zinc removes both bromines and the carbon chain closes: BrCH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>Br + Zn &rarr; cyclopropane + ZnBr<sub>2</sub> (Freund reaction). 1,2-Dibromopropane with zinc would give propene.</p>', 'Vicinal dihalides give alkenes; 1,3-dihalides give rings.'));
A(mk(2, 'chlorination of benzene', 'Benzene reacts with excess chlorine in bright sunlight to give', 'hexachlorocyclohexane (BHC)', ['chlorobenzene', 'hexachlorobenzene', '1,4-dichlorobenzene'], '<p>Without a Lewis acid and in strong light, chlorine adds across the ring through a free-radical path: C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>6</sub> + 3Cl<sub>2</sub> &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>6</sub>Cl<sub>6</sub> (gammexane, lindane is one isomer). With FeCl<sub>3</sub> in the dark you get substitution.</p>', 'Catalyst gives substitution; light gives addition.'));

